Your ingress setup is fine. The problem is your AWS account: AWS is refusing to create the load balancer (OperationNotPermitted: This AWS account currently does not support creating load balancers). So the controller pod is running, but the Service will stay <pending> forever.

The error mentions Elastic Load Balancing v2. That means it tried to create an NLB, because the AWS manifest adds the aws-load-balancer-type: nlb annotation. Your k8s-2 setup got a Classic ELB (a95a9...-1102760794.ap-south-1.elb.amazonaws.com in App.tsx:10 is the Classic address format). So the block might only cover the newer load balancer types. That's worth trying first.

Option 1: Ask for a Classic ELB instead of an NLB
Remove the NLB annotation; the trailing - deletes it:


kubectl annotate svc ingress-nginx-controller -n ingress-nginx service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-type-
kubectl get svc -n ingress-nginx -w
If EXTERNAL-IP fills in with an *.elb.amazonaws.com name after a minute or two, you're done. If kubectl describe svc shows the same OperationNotPermitted error, the block covers all load balancer types. Then go to Option 2.

Option 2: Skip the load balancer and use a NodePort

kubectl patch svc ingress-nginx-controller -n ingress-nginx --type merge -p '{\"spec\":{\"type\":\"NodePort\",\"externalTrafficPolicy\":\"Cluster\"}}'
kubectl get svc -n ingress-nginx      # http port should stay 32448
kubectl get nodes -o wide             # note a node's EXTERNAL-IP
Then, in the EC2 console, add an inbound rule to the worker nodes' security group: TCP 32448 from your IP. You can then reach the app at http://<node-external-ip>:32448/. The \" escaping is needed because PowerShell 5.1 strips plain quotes out of the JSON.

Getting the block lifted
This is a restriction AWS puts on the account, often on new or unverified accounts. No Kubernetes change can remove it. Open a case with AWS Support (Account and billing → service limits) and ask them to enable Elastic Load Balancing.

Two problems you'll hit next, whichever option you use
/api will return 404. Your Ingress forwards /api/... to the backend unchanged, but index.ts only handles /. You'll need a rewrite on the Ingress or routes under /api in the backend.
The frontend still calls the old k8s-2 backend load balancer. That URL is hardcoded at App.tsx:10 and App.tsx:25, and that load balancer is probably gone. Behind the ingress it should call /api instead, and you'll need to rebuild the frontend image after the change.
